      "textEn": "146\n\nMeaning: According to Srila Sridhar Swami, this verse describes the conduct of a devotee of God. The activities of a pure devotee are free from material envy, false pride, illusion, and lust. According to Vaishnava commentators, the word 'Bijanam' in this verse refers to desires, or strong wishes, which gradually result in those\nactions, for which the soul becomes a slave to the fruits. In this way, work-deeds-\nThe compound word 'Bijanam' indicates the deep-rooted desire, from which sexual pleasure and sexual-\nThe enjoyment of the expanded means of pleasure, through which dominance is established over the physical world, which\nmentioned in Bhagwat (5.5.8) - 'Grihasthasyaaptavittai' - beautiful residence, good food for filling the stomach\nsufficient land to produce and children, friends, social contact and abundant bank savings in the form of\nhappened. To completely forget that he is a perpetual servant of God, all these\nmaterial things are necessary.\nTherefore, as it is said in the Bhagavata—Janasyamohoyam aham\nMameeti—The bound soul, deluded by these objects of material illusion, becomes convinced that he\nis the center of the universe, and every object in the world is made for his sense gratification. Whoever is such\nthat becomes an obstacle in the illusory enjoyment, he immediately becomes his enemy and remains eager to kill. Due to this egoistic-intellect and the noose of Maya, the entire world is being badly disturbed by the conflict arising from envy and lust. The only possible solution is that the leadership of the pure devotees of God described here should be accepted. \"Corruption spreads through power, and the supreme authority\ncompletely corrupts.\" This saying, although valid in the physical world, is especially mentioned here that a pure devotee who takes refuge in the lotus feet of God cannot even think of joining material envy and\nIndriya Tripti (the satisfaction of the senses).\nHis mind always remains pure and profound.\nAnd he is aware of the ultimate welfare of every living being. The Krishna consciousness movement is trying its best to tell the suffering of the earth, the beings who are suffering, that the ultimate\nneed of the mind is in human society. A brain distorted by fever cannot properly direct, and if society's\nso-called intellectuals are burning with selfish thoughts, then they are suffering from a feverish, diseased, and afflicted brain"
