      "textEn": "162\n\nAnd the reasons are not different. Therefore, the glorification and listening to the glory of Krishna are the cause of divine bliss.\nTherefore, they are the cause. King Nimi said to the yogis, “You are great saints absorbed in the love of God. You are speaking of Maya, but the meaning would be Krishna bhava naamrut. Don't think that\nyou have already told me everything. The intoxicating nectar of your teachings has made me eager to listen more and more about God.”\n\nKing Nimi was also a great devotee of God, otherwise there was no question of conversing with great men like Nau Yogendra.\nThere was no question. But as a humble Vaishnava, he considered himself to be covered by material titles,\na common bound soul. In this way, he expressed curiosity to know the true nature of Maya,\nso that he could avoid future attempts by Maya to throw him into the fire of worldly existence. Shri Antariksha said:\nThese beings, the soul of beings, the great elements, the great beings.\nSasarjochavchanyadhyaah swamatratmaprakhyataye. 3. Meaning of the word.\n\nShri-Antariksh said—Shri Antariksh said; Ebhih—by these (physical elements); Bhutani—animals; Bhut-Atma—all\nThe soul of the creation; Maha-Bhuta-Iha—by the elements of the great element; Maha-Bhuja—Oh mighty-armed king; Sasraj—He created; Uchcha-\nAvachani—both high and low; Adi—the first; Swa—of one's own parts; Matra—sensory gratification; Atma—and self-\nRealization; Prakhyaye—to facilitate; Prakhyaye—for the sake of well-being. Shri Antariksha said: O mighty-armed king, by making the material elements active, the original soul has created beings in higher and lower species, so that these\nbounded souls can practice sensory gratification or ultimate liberation according to their own wishes. Meaning: According to Srila Vishwanath Chakravarti Thakur, in this verse, the description of Maya is through its powers,\nthat is, through the description of its qualities.\nIn the Bhagavad Gita, Lord Krishna has called Maya Gunamayi, meaning\n“endowed with material qualities.” In this verse, the physical qualities are described in a veiled manner, meaning\n“high and low species”. Just as diverse wombs manifest, similarly,\nin them, beauty, ugliness, strength, weakness, etc., qualities are found in proportion to the qualities of nature. As confirmed in the Bhagavad Gita (13.22)—Causation, qualities, and their collection"
